 implication is true. But since we have to consider all conditions together, maybe there are constraints from other parts?\n\nThird condition: \"R假\" which means R is false. In the output given, R is indeed false. So that's satisfied.\n\nWait a second, but how do these three statements interact? Let me think of them as logical premises:\n\nPremise 1: (P ∧ Q) ∨ (¬P ∧ ¬Q) → Exactly one of P or Q is true.\n\nPremise 2: If Q is true then R is true. So Q → R.\n\nPremise 3: R is false. So R = false.\n\nWait, but if premise 3 says R is false, then that directly contradicts premise 2 unless Q is also false. Because if Q were true, then from premise 2 we must have R true, but premise 3 says R is false. Therefore, the only way all three premises can be true is if Q is false (so premise 2's antecedent is false) and R is indeed false as per premise 3.\n\nSo let me formalize this:\n\nWe need to find truth values for P, Q, R such that:\n\n1. Exactly one of P or Q is true.\n2. If Q is true then R must be true (Q → R).\n3. R is false (R = false).\n\nLet's see possible cases from premise 1 first.\n\nCase 1: P is true, Q is false.\n\nThen check the other premises:\n\n- Premise 2: Since Q is false, the implication Q→R is automatically true regardless of R.\n- Premise 3 requires R to be false. So if we set R = false here, then all three are satisfied. That gives P=true, Q=false, R=false which matches the desired output.\n\nCase 2: Q is true, P is false.\n\nNow check premises:\n\n- Premise 2 says Q→R must hold. Since Q is true here, this requires R to be true.\n- But premise 3 states that R must be false. So in this case, we have a contradiction because R can't be both true and false. Therefore, this scenario is impossible.\n\nTherefore the only possible solution is Case 1 where P=true, Q=false, R=false. Which is exactly what's required. \n\nWait, but let me make sure there are no other interpretations of the problem statement. The original Chinese might have a slightly different structure. Let me parse it again:\n\n\"P与Q恰好一个真；Q真则R真；R假。输出 P=true,Q=false,R=false。\"\n\nBreaking down each part separated by semicolons:\n\n1. \"P与Q恰好一个真\" → Exactly one of P or Q is true.\n\n2. \"Q真则R真\" → If Q is true then R is true (this is a conditional statement).\n\n3. \"R假\" → R is false (a declarative sentence stating R's truth value).\n\nSo the three statements are:\n\n1. Exactly one of P, Q is true.\n\n2. Q implies R.\n\n3. R is false.\n\nTherefore combining these, as above, only Case 1 works because in that case, since Q is false, the implication holds (no problem), and R can be false without contradiction. In the other case where Q is true, we must have R true from premise 2, but premise 3 says R is false → impossible.\n\nTherefore the answer given is correct: P=true, Q=false, R=false.",
